 ## Overview
-Back-face rasterized volume rendering is a GPU-accelerated technique that determines ray entry and exit points for volume ray casting by rasterizing the volume's bounding box geometry rather than computing intersections analytically in the fragment shader [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently restricting fragment shader execution to pixels intersecting the volume while correctly handling camera zoom levels where front faces might project behind the camera [S2, unofficial]. It differs from [Bounding Box Raymarching](2071-bounding-box-raymarching.md) by explicitly utilizing a two-pass approach where the first pass captures back-face coordinates into a texture, and the second pass uses these coordinates to define ray endpoints for front-to-back compositing [S2, S3, unofficial]. Unlike [Volume Ray Casting with RayData Texture](3077-volume-ray-casting-with-raydata-texture.md), which stores ray data in a dedicated texture, this method often computes ray directions from geometry attributes or camera matrices directly in the fragment shader [S5, unofficial].
+Back-face rasterized volume rendering is a GPU-accelerated technique that determines ray entry and exit points for volume ray casting by rasterizing the back faces of the volume's bounding box geometry [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently culling pixels outside the volume and correctly handling camera zooming, which can cause front-face clipping issues [S2, unofficial]. It differs from [Bounding Box Raymarching](2071-bounding-box-raymarching.md) by explicitly utilizing a two-pass approach where the first pass captures back-face coordinates into a texture, and the second pass uses these coordinates to define ray endpoints for front-to-back compositing [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. Unlike [Volume Ray Casting with RayData Texture](3077-volume-ray-casting-with-raydata-texture.md), which stores ray data in a dedicated texture, this method often computes ray directions from geometry attributes or camera matrices directly in the fragment shader [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method employs a two-pass rendering pipeline. The first pass renders the back faces of the volume's axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) to a Framebuffer Object (FBO) attached to a 2D texture, storing the 3D entry coordinates. The second pass renders the front faces, sampling the stored entry points from the texture and combining them with the current front-face position to define the ray segment for marching through the volume data [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
+The method uses a two-pass rendering pipeline. The first pass renders the back faces of the volume's bounding box to a 2D texture to store the ray entry (or exit, depending on culling direction) points. The second pass renders the front faces, retrieves the stored points, and performs the ray marching integration [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
 
