+### [Volume Ray Casting with RayData Texture](3077-volume-ray-casting-with-raydata-texture.md)
+Stores ray data in a dedicated texture, differing from back-face rasterization which derives endpoints from geometry attributes.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$, where $N$ is the number of samples per ray and $P$ is the number of pixels covered by the volume's bounding box. The two-pass approach adds a constant overhead for the back-face render pass [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \cdot H)$ for the back-face texture, where $W$ and $H$ are the viewport dimensions. The texture stores 4 floats (RGBA16F) per pixel [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Avoids expensive ray-box intersection calculations in the fragment shader for exit determination, at the cost of an extra render pass [S4, unofficial]. It restricts fragment processing to pixels touched by the volume, which is efficient when the volume does not cover the entire screen [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Correctly handles zooming into the volume without clipping issues; simplifies ray boundary determination; leverages hardware rasterization for culling [S2, S4, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires two passes; the back-face texture consumes additional memory; fixed step size may lead to aliasing if not adjusted for ray direction and voxel size [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When hardware ray-box intersection is not available or too expensive, and correct zoom behavior is required.
-*   **When not to use:** When memory bandwidth is a critical bottleneck due to the extra texture read/write, or when single-pass rendering is strictly required (consider [GPU Multi-Volume Ray Casting Mapper](2461-gpu-multi-volume-ray-casting-mapper.md) or [3D Pixel Texture Classification](6055-3d-pixel-texture-classification.md)).
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot W \cdot H)$, where $N$ is the number of samples per ray, and $W \times H$ is the resolution. The two-pass approach adds a constant overhead.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \cdot H)$ for the back-face texture storage [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Efficiently culls pixels outside the volume, reducing fragment shader invocations [S2, unofficial]. Performance scales with resolution and step count.
+*   **Strengths:** Handles zooming correctly; avoids complex ray-box intersection calculations in the fragment shader for boundary determination [S2, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires two render passes; extra memory for the intermediate texture [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originated as a practical solution for GPU-based volume rendering in early OpenGL and WebGL environments, where explicit ray-box intersection in fragment shaders was either unavailable or inefficient. It was popularized in tutorials and open-source implementations such as `levl-dev/VolumeRayCasting_0` [S1, unofficial], `thePZH/VolumeRendering-OpenGL` [S4, unofficial], and WebGL volume rendering guides [S2, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of naive ray casting by using the graphics pipeline's built-in face culling to determine ray bounds.
+The method is a standard technique in GPU-accelerated volume rendering, leveraging the graphics pipeline's rasterization capabilities to determine ray boundaries. It is commonly implemented in WebGL and OpenGL contexts [S1, S2, S4, unofficial].
 
